     "A page renames with a file beside it for a property its page type does not declare, and the only thing telling `rename-page` that key holds a file is the search across page types in `shapedIn`. Asking the page's type instead leaves that file behind under the old name while the page moves. So the search cannot go until it is settled whether a rename owes a file the declarations do not name, and this is the last of its callers: the declarations and both file-property warrants no longer need it.",
   evidence:
-    '`besideIn` in `rename-page.refactor-change.code.ts` reads each key the page states, asks `schemaOf` for the page property of that name, and moves the file beside the page where the answer is a file property. `schemaOf` handed a name with no page type reads every page type in the index and takes whichever carries a page of that slug.\n\n`ec8f203c31` had it ask the page\'s own type, through `propertiesIfNamedOf(held.pageTypeSlug, ...)`, matching the key against the declared `propertySlug`. Seven of the eight tests held. The eighth, `rename-page.refactor-change.test.ts:126`, states a module carrying `testFixtures` where the type it is indexed under declares `code` and `test` alone, and the file `wide.module.test-fixtures.ts` stopped being carried. `407f0abd` put the search back for this caller.\n\nThe fixture is not thin by accident: `aType("2", "module", ["domain"], ["code", "test"])` in `indexing.module.test-fixtures.ts` is what the test repo holds, while the tree\'s own `module` page type declares `file-property/test-fixtures` at `module.page-type.ts:24`. So the case is a page whose type has since narrowed, or a page written against a type it does not match. Either way the file is on disk and the page is moving.\n\nWhat has to be decided is what a rename owes such a file. Carrying it means reaching a file property by key alone, which the declarations cannot answer and the search can. Leaving it means the file keeps the old page\'s name with no page at that name, which is a file orphaned by a mechanical change rather than by anyone\'s decision.\n\nThe other three callers are settled. `declared-properties.module.code.ts:70` is handed a `pagePropertySlug`, and all 2419 of those name their page type now. Both file-property warrants ask the page\'s type as of `484d2b09`, measured over 5078 paths: no path warranted less, 1713 swapping the page the search picked for the page the type declares, 540 gaining a warrant the search refused for naming more than one page.\n\nA second copy of the same search sits at `declared-properties.module.code.ts:240` as `schemaAmong`, over an in-memory map rather than the index, and the test fixtures across the tree read through it. Both copies go together or neither does.',
